      "content": "Ah, I see where the confusion lies. It's not that the vocal folds are being pushed apart by the air pressure; rather, it's the air pressure that causes the vocal folds to come together and vibrate. When you produce the voiced sound /z/, the air pressure from your lungs forces the vocal folds to come together, causing them to vibrate and produce the buzzing sound.\n\nAs for feeling the vibration with your fingers on your throat, it's not a reliable method to check if you're doing it right. The vibration of the vocal folds is subtle and can be difficult to feel, especially for beginners. It's more effective to listen to yourself and compare your pronunciation to a native speaker or use a recording device to check your pronunciation.\n\nHowever, if you're interested in feeling the vibrations, you can try placing your fingers gently on your throat while producing the /z/ sound. You should feel a slight vibration or fluttering sensation as the vocal folds vibrate. Remember to be gentle and not press too hard, as the vocal folds are delicate and can be easily irritated.",
